{"call_id":"call_SBbKYyZLhKByvnziwIkeCFUA","event_id":"ctco_01a09041-eb20-73d2-a6f6-ae3481cdecd8","output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"e7dda3\",\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":2488,\"output\":\"# Realistic live-key scale benchmarks\\n\\nMeasured on an Apple M5 Max with 128 GiB RAM, macOS 26.5.1. Release build of the git-meta 0.1.13 workspace. These are actual repositories and timings, not extrapolated latency estimates.\\n\\n![Storage and operation costs](realistic-live-scale.svg)\\n\\n## Fixture shape\\n\\nThe [observed GitButler month and adoption model](realistic-month.md) is replayed in 27-day cohorts, retaining all live metadata. Commit IDs are rehashed and branch/change targets namespaced after the first cohort. Project/path metadata is reused. Transcript roles, message counts, tool structure, and sizes come from the frozen local-session profiles; content is synthetic and shuffled per cohort. Public PR/issue payload templates remain reusable. This preserves the heavy tail, but is not a prediction of future activity or compression ratios.\\n\\nA logical key is one `(target, key)` pair. List entries, set members, historical rewrites, source commits, and metadata commits are counted separately. The seed has 34 distinct key names. Checkpoints stop at the exact requested key count, potentially partway through a lifecycle publication. No retention is applied while building them.\\n\\n| Metric | 100,000 | 1,000,000 | 5,000,000 |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Live logical keys | 100,000 | 1,000,000 | 5,000,000 |\\n| Source commit targets | 6,699 | 67,300 | 336,637 |\\n| Branch targets | 2,310 | 23,037 | 115,159 |\\n| Change-ID targets | 469 | 4,711 | 23,565 |\\n| Metadata commits, actual revision walk | 43,407 | 435,006 | 2,175,521 |\\n| Historical key operations | 121,695 | 1,217,774 | 6,089,372 |\\n| Live list entries | 124,529 | 1,236,326 | 6,185,873 |\\n| Live set members | 34,436 | 343,086 | 1,714,836 |\\n| Payload GiB | 0.246 | 2.433 | 12.206 |\\n| Git object files GiB, whole history | 0.208 | 2.498 | 9.202 |\\n| Producer SQLite GiB | 0.408 | 4.065 | 20.415 |\\n| Transcript share of payload | 86.0% | 85.9% | 86.0% |\\n| Simulated end date (UTC) | 2027-02-02 | 2031-07-30 | 2051-06-25 |\\n\\nThe long simulated timeline is deliberate: millions of retained keys at the observed monthly activity rate represent many years of accumulation. A larger team or richer attachment policy would reach the same key count sooner. It is a scale fixture, not a claim that a future GitButler project would retain identical habits.\\n\\n## Reads and writes\\n\\n500 sampled scalar reads, 500 complete-transcript reads, and 500 scalar Git-tree lookups per tier. 100 updates to sampled scalar keys measure store writes and serialization separately on a copied fixture. The OS cache is warm; each batch reuses one Session, samples are sequential, and the first operation is included. These are library operation timings, not fresh CLI invocation timings. Values below are median / p95 milliseconds.\\n\\n| Operation (median / p95 ms) | 100,000 | 1,000,000 | 5,000,000 |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Scalar read | 0.163 / 0.243 | 0.290 / 0.604 | 0.326 / 0.404 |\\n| Complete transcript read | 0.378 / 1.471 | 0.709 / 2.856 | 0.794 / 1.920 |\\n| Git tree scalar lookup | 0.048 / 0.064 | 0.075 / 0.195 | 0.622 / 0.961 |\\n| Store write, default | 0.328 / 0.555 | 0.525 / 0.845 | 0.774 / 1.134 |\\n| Serialize, default | 15.938 / 18.910 | 141.571 / 162.191 | 675.297 / 708.544 |\\n| Store write, ANALYZE | 0.109 / 0.256 | 0.136 / 0.264 | 0.148 / 0.186 |\\n| Serialize, ANALYZE | 3.053 / 4.539 | 582.113 / 631.381 | 3075.853 / 3104.859 |\\n\\nLargest observed default serialization: 100,000 keys: 615.8 ms; 1,000,000 keys: 6536.6 ms; 5,000,000 keys: 32591.3 ms. Full latency summaries, including maxima for every operation, are in the JSON results.\\n\\nThe default one-target serialization query chooses `idx_metadata_source_ref`, scanning local metadata. `ANALYZE` enables the planner to choose `idx_metadata_target` instead. Exact query plans are in the JSON results. Generation refreshes metadata-table statistics every 5,000 publications to make construction practical, but clears them on checkpoint copies. The default and analyzed write measurements therefore use distinct copies. The initial 100k generation predates this maintenance change; generation time is not presented as a controlled scale comparison. Full ANALYZE can also make the modification-log query scan its whole lookup index at larger scales. Generation therefore limits statistics to the metadata table after the 600k checkpoint; the benchmark still measures full ANALYZE separately. After 1.2M live keys, construction object maintenance changes from every 5,000 to every 50,000 metadata commits, with maintenance at every checkpoint. This changes pack layout and can affect physical storage, but all operation measurements use the frozen library build. After 1.8M keys, generation buffers objects in gix memory and writes native packs every 5,000 publications; the final 5M history is fully repacked. A 16k-key comparison reproduces exactly the same complete 6,816-commit history. The generator exposes the existing Session::from_repo constructor to configure object storage; measurement algorithms and binaries remain unchanged.\\n\\n## Fresh materialization and correctness\\n\\n| Metric | 100,000 | 1,000,000 | 5,000,000 |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Unpruned materialization seconds | 8.07 | 80.06 | 405.74 |\\n| Recreated keys | 100,000 | 1,000,000 | 5,000,000 |\\n| Missing target/key identities | 139 | 1,262 | 6,329 |\\n| Unexpected target/key identities | 139 | 1,262 | 6,329 |\\n| Sampled value mismatches | 4 | 4 | 2 |\\n\\n**Matching total row counts does not establish a successful round trip.** The existing slash-containing branch target parser can interpret a path prefix as a complete target when an 8-bit shard prefix collides. The benchmark compares every target/key identity in both directions and up to 1,000 sampled values. Misaddressed keys are a correctness failure; materialization timings must be read with that limitation. The consumer uses a local object-store alternate, so these times exclude object transfer.\\n\\n## Retention\\n\\nEach DB-rebuild retention test starts from the unpruned tip. Project keys survive regardless of age. Timings cover rebuilding the filtered tree, excluding before/after tree-size traversal. This path reads all metadata before filtering, as the CLI does, so its work grows even when the retained tip stays near 50k keys. Retention uses the fixture simulated time consistently. Git history is retained; pruning the tip does not reclaim all historical storage.\\n\\n| Starting live keys | Window | Retained logical keys | Tip payload MiB | Rebuild seconds |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| 100,000 | 30d | 16,300 | 38.36 | 2.00 |\\n| 100,000 | 90d | 50,680 | 134.87 | 1.11 |\\n| 100,000 | 1y | 100,000 | 252.10 | 1.49 |\\n| 1,000,000 | 30d | 16,060 | 39.05 | 19.67 |\\n| 1,000,000 | 90d | 48,928 | 117.19 | 15.49 |\\n| 1,000,000 | 1y | 198,468 | 488.85 | 18.57 |\\n| 5,000,000 | 30d | 16,212 | 39.16 | 143.59 |\\n| 5,000,000 | 90d | 50,013 | 134.24 | 115.40 |\\n| 5,000,000 | 1y | 201,757 | 510.17 | 99.93 |\\n\\nThe legacy routine labels serialized blob-path counts as “keys”; those o